artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a tumor modeled as a population composed of two phenotypic compartments: drug-sensitive cells with density S(t) and drug-resistant cells with density R(t), evolving in continuous time t >= 0. Cells switch phenotype reversibly with rates k_{SR}(t) (sensitive -> resistant) and k_{RS}(t) (resistant -> sensitive), each of which may depend on whether the drug is being administered. Under a time-varying treatment schedule u(t) in {0,1}, the dynamics are

  dS/dt = r_S S (1 - (S+R)/K) - d(u) S - k_{SR}(u) S + k_{RS}(u) R,
  dR/dt = r_R R (1 - (S+R)/K) - k_{RS}(u) R + k_{SR}(u) S,

where r_S, r_R > 0 are intrinsic growth rates, K > 0 is the carrying capacity, and d(u) >= 0 is the drug-induced death rate of sensitive cells (with d(0)=0). The total tumor burden is N(t) = S(t)+R(t), and an adaptive therapy protocol is a feedback rule that switches u(t) based on observations of N(t).

Problem. Develop a mathematical framework that prescribes, as a function of the parameters (r_S, r_R, K, d, k_{SR}(0), k_{SR}(1), k_{RS}(0), k_{RS}(1)) and patient-level uncertainty in these parameters:
  (a) necessary and sufficient conditions under which an adaptive (feedback) therapy protocol yields a longer time-to-progression (e.g. time until N(t) exceeds a fixed threshold N*) than continuous maximum-tolerated-dose therapy;
  (b) optimal rules for triggering treatment holidays that exploit reversible switching to re-sensitize the resistant compartment, including quantitative bounds on the switching rates k_{RS}(0) for which holidays are beneficial;
  (c) an identifiability analysis specifying which longitudinal measurements of N(t) (and possibly biomarkers of S, R) suffice to estimate the plasticity parameters of an individual tumor before deciding to apply adaptive therapy;
  (d) the extent to which drug-induced (rather than purely selected) resistance, modeled by k_{SR}(1) > k_{SR}(0), alters the answers in (a)-(c).
